186060796 #edb/ref 1061 45528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060796 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cc528712-4193-5c9b-a419-12840d9e8601" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060796 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060797 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060797 #edb/ref 1061 45529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060797 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d411433f-4c7a-586b-ac35-14040fe93360" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060797 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060798 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060798 #edb/ref 1061 45530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060798 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "381d3128-fbcb-5ce6-9e0e-a3e655f68eda" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060798 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060799 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060799 #edb/ref 1061 45533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060799 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1b88504d-3636-5915-a46b-2cb099cdbcd4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060799 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060800 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060800 #edb/ref 1061 45536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060800 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "532a36f2-e1c7-5155-9100-a6dfba320681" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060800 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060801 #edb/ref 1061 45539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060801 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fa2869ca-219e-5a9b-ba35-80d3636e0f6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060801 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060977 #edb/ref 1064 "The unit circle provides a convenient way to represent the sine and cosine of special angles in each quadrant. \n\nWe first recall the following:\n\n- The ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-coordinate of any point on the unit circle equals the *cosine* of the corresponding central angle, and\n\n- the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinate equals the *sine* of the corresponding central angle.\n\nLet's use the unit circle to list the special values of sine and cosine, starting with the first quadrant. First, we mark off the special angles in this quadrant.\n\n![](images/3a/3a0c6eb5bc286272ef67aee5ef0fa625a6f4e44635413ca43060efc9082b1848.png)\n\nNow, let's write down each point's ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)- and ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinates, corresponding to the cosine and sine of each point's central angle. In addition, we'll highlight the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinate at each point.\n\n![](images/6b/6bb6bb7cc95a17c605056e1e1e1ae27bc8cf2f124e419c5e6b9810a72b950508.png)\n\nBefore we move on, observe that if we rotate counterclockwise from the positive ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis, we see that the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinates at the special angles are given by the following sequence:\n![](images/ef/ef3998f9651e37dba31e1597a503577243def17e903dbeb5322ab434a4815b3c.svg) \n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/ae/ae75322c270db13d7ee5ba6079b236b9a18d463f74cc3e5ee70b6fe113d224e3.png)\n\nThere is a similar pattern for the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-coordinates (corresponding to the cosine of the central angle). The only difference is that now we go in reverse, from ![](images/26/2614c435bff188ded3027f2a8f1ea011b37b41a1d62d2ff4bae181ce9107b5c2.svg) to ![](images/f1/f12b2ec86446e22fc9d1c982c156c7d089a97fecca1670f416ecfd63cf5899d3.svg) as shown below \n\n![](images/2a/2af7f2c7cf14c017a545a4144ed88dffe7b7f7bfb6e13a2b6144552ef3cfaa6c.png)\n\nTake a moment to ensure you can reproduce this diagram on your own without looking. We'll add to it shortly." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060977 #edb/ref 1065 7903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060977 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "307aa9da-5076-5fd6-8a9c-ad92348c8d93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060977 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060978 #edb/ref 1064 "So, we know how the unit circle can be used to list the cosine and sine of the special angles in the *first* quadrant. But what about the other quadrants? \n\nThe values in the remaining quadrants can be obtained from the coordinates in the first quadrant. Let's see how:\n\n- To obtain the points in the *second* quadrant, let's first mark off the special angles in this quadrant. These angles have the same reference angles as the angles in the first quadrant:\n\n- $180^\\circ - 30^\\circ = 150^\\circ$\n\n- $180^\\circ - 45^\\circ = 135^\\circ$\n\n- $180^\\circ - 60^\\circ = 120^\\circ$\n\nWe mark these angles below and include the quadrantal angle $180^\\circ.$\n\n![](images/49/49105a62c4eecffeb32f6777ddf98022c5ca46079a33294e7eeafc9164444cba.png)\n\nNow, we reflect the coordinates in the first quadrant in the $y$-axis. This changes the sign of the $x$-coordinate at each point and leaves the $y$-coordinate unchanged.\n\n![](images/e4/e44980d06903b6570182b99787ca7dec8cceacab2f72dce04fc48b3d7c660059.png)\n\n- To obtain the points in the third quadrant, we first find the special angles in this quadrant. Again, these angles have the same reference angles as the special angles in the first quadrant:\n\n- $180^\\circ + 30^\\circ = 210^\\circ$\n\n- $180^\\circ + 45^\\circ = 225^\\circ$\n\n- $180^\\circ + 60^\\circ = 240^\\circ$\n\nThen, we take the points in the second quadrant and reflect them in the $x$-axis. This changes the sign of the $y$-coordinate at each point and leaves the $x$-coordinate unchanged.\n\n![](images/bb/bbfcf14e39ee122cf1bd7048903ac7f205325c77d4b2899c53619495ffeb8d8c.png)\n\n- Finally, to obtain the points in the fourth quadrant, we first compute the special angles in this quadrant:\n\n- $360^\\circ - 30^\\circ = 330^\\circ$\n\n- $360^\\circ - 45^\\circ = 315^\\circ$\n\n- $360^\\circ - 60^\\circ = 300^\\circ$\n\nThen, we take the points in the first quadrant and reflect them in the $x$-axis. This changes the sign of the $y$-coordinate at each point and leaves the $x$-coordinate unchanged.\n\n![](images/f5/f507964e8fe06cc54ceab447d570bb004b4e9a2b0b8fa3074762e28a41009a2a.png)\n\nTherefore, our complete unit circle is as follows.\n\n![](images/f5/f5006a5a3c363f83e6d4624fc72fefa140e29ef6ef23db16fbab5538b421a058.png)\n\nTake a moment to ensure you can create this diagram yourself without looking. We'll be referring to it often!" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060978 #edb/ref 1065 7904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060978 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f3691d0c-59fd-5cd5-8b22-f20733d06f4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060978 #edb/ref 1067 "A Method For Constructing the Other Quadrants" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233307 #edb/ref 1069 "q-123598" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233307 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233307 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\operatorname{sin}{210}^{∘}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233307 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c4fe9757-2d60-5e00-a868-aa48063d0b65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233307 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${210}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}{210}^{∘}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233313 #edb/ref 1069 "q-123608" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233313 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233313 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233313 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "84a0eb74-4e3b-5b80-807e-ccf4f3502fe5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233313 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233324 #edb/ref 1069 "q-123638" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233324 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233324 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233324 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2d02f521-e8ed-522a-a329-1f6d01f412b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233324 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}=\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234174 #edb/ref 1069 "q-64929" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234174 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234174 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234174 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "52fdb8a8-7b9e-5f48-95b6-5c145626cfac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234174 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/a3/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569486 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569486 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569486 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1dc11e74-5683-5e8d-80d8-76b11e1ef5b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569495 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569495 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569495 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "243136ce-4a0a-57c9-a550-54229f41104f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569679 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569679 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569679 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "95626cd5-58bf-5b05-9d17-272dc4d286b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569723 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569723 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569723 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b2225633-3982-500e-aceb-598ad2c9dd69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569743 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569743 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569743 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bb36dd47-9d34-5a02-9fba-9e0b0e7b2af1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569773 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569773 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569773 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d22384f1-7ee3-57c8-b472-368fa995344e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569778 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569778 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569778 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d59e1f90-02b8-5b16-90e7-10a8e98457fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569787 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569787 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569787 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "da706140-bf28-56bb-9b3d-9c80fbbb5352" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569790 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569790 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569790 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "dbf553dd-432a-5db7-9330-1fe86b00d67e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569832 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569832 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569832 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f93b95d6-53f4-5c55-b1c0-20f5b8ed7c69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569899 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186569486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569899 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186569495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569899 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186569679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569899 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186569743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569899 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186569773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569899 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569899 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569899 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186569773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569899 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8fd4e1c0-3680-5d3a-9d0f-c3d351751bcd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569905 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186569723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569905 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186569778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569905 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186569787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569905 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186569790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569905 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186569832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569905 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569905 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569905 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186569790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569905 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9ad166a8-68f6-52e5-aeab-c9f3b40cf4fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570015 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186569899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570015 #edb/ref 1069 "q-123595" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570015 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570015 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570015 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a462c56c-120f-5159-b0b8-404f14801fe3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570015 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${45}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570027 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186569905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570027 #edb/ref 1069 "q-1696" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570027 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570027 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570027 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d25800d2-aaa9-5069-8bc6-84b04968eef5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186570027 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
